          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0, 1, 2, \ldots
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} partially ordered
            by the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/divisibility+relation"} divisibility relation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a|b
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b = a k
            for some natural number
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice"} lattice
            %em greatest common divisor
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b
            is their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meet"} meet
            in this lattice.
          %h2#remarks Remarks
          %h3#in_the_natural_numbers In the natural numbers
          %p
            Spelled out, this means that the greatest common divisor of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b \in \mathbb{N}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi gcd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gcd(a, b)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in \mathbb{N}
            uniquely characterized by the following two conditions:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d|a
                and
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi d
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d|b
            %li
              %p
                if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c|a
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c|b
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi d
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c|d
          %p
            One could equivalently equip the natural numbers with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi gcd
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gcd:\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}
            which satisfies the two conditions:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi gcd
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gcd(a, b)|a
                and
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi gcd
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gcd(a, b)|b
            %li
              %p
                if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c|a
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c|b
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi gcd
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c|\gcd(a, b)
          %p
            It is almost but not quite true that “greatest” means greatest with respect to the usual ordering
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            is the maximal element with respect to the divisibility ordering, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi gcd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gcd(0, 0) = 0
            according to the definition above. However, there is no “greatest” common divisor of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            with itself if we construe “greatest” in the sense of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            with itself, and there is no
            = succeed "-greatest" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
          %p Thus, the convention often seen in textbooks, which replaces the second condition above with
          %ul
            %li
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c|a
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c|b
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \leq d
          %p or
          %ul
            %li
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c|a
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c|b
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi gcd
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \leq \gcd(a, b)
          %p
            is slightly more awkward, and certainly less “pure” (mixing two relations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi gcd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} gcd
            is at bottom an
            = succeed "-theoretic" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideal"} ideal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+ideal+domain"} principal ideal domain
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
            whose posetal collapse is the collection of ideals, ordered oppositely to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inclusion"} inclusion
            = succeed "-theoretic" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leq
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gaussian+integers"} Gaussian integers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi gcd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} gcd
            still makes perfectly good sense if we use the first formulation above, expressed purely in terms of divisibility.
          %p
            From the point of view of principal ideals in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pid"} pid
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B%C3%A9zout+domain"} Bézout domain
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join"} join
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi gcd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\gcd(a, b)) = (a) + (b)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+algorithm"} Euclidean algorithm
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+domains"} Euclidean domains
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a) + (b)
            which consists of
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
          %h3#in_the_integers In the integers
          %p
            In the set of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prelattice"} prelattice
            rather than a lattice, because the divisibility relation is only a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
            rather than a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} partial order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+of+units"} group of units
            of the integers.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/least+common+multiple"} least common multiple
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermite+normal+form"} Hermite normal form
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coprime+integers"} coprime integers
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GCD+ring"} GCD ring
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greatest_common_divisor"} Greatest common divisor
